# Round sparkroom (kind profile): ws+Spark against ws alone when the workstation's host room is smaller than the model —
# the configuration the Spark is for. The box's 256 GB holds V4.1-Flash Q3_K_M (324 GB) with the A6000's 48 GB; a
# workstation with 190 GB of host room (BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=190G: 48 + 190 = 238 of the 324) cannot, and pages the
# missing 86 GB of experts from NVMe each token (BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=90G), while the same box with the Spark holds them
# on the node (BLOOMERY_CARD_BUDGET=90G, tier 94.65 GB: 48 + 190 + 95 = 333 >= 324).
# [derived, rig-log tools/spark-model.py decode_bigger_quant with 92 GB past the box] ws alone: the missing share of
# the routed experts read from NVMe at QD ~1-4 (2.5-5.7 GB/s), the hot half kept by residency: 7 tok/s at 50 % hot,
# 4 at 0 %, 13 at 75 %. ws+Spark: the lock sitting's 37 tok/s(p50) (the node round trip 7 ms a step; the host set
# is smaller than at plan a, so the host leg hides). The ratio is the number: 3..9 x.
kind: profile
question: with the host room capped at 190G so the model does not fit the workstation, how much faster is the same
  workstation with the Spark holding the missing experts than alone paging them from NVMe?
term: tok/s @ n=96, prose P 512, depth 512, A6000 stage; both arms BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=190G; the plain arm --place a
  with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=90G (the nvtier takes the rest) and BLOOMERY_R8=off (the NVMe tier refuses a paged plan under
  the r8 sidecar by name: `GpuModel::nvme_tier`; the host leg then reads the source tiles, slower than the node arm's r8
  path by a term under the paging), the node arm --place bp with the Spark tier over tcp (BLOOMERY_TIER_NODES,
  BLOOMERY_TIER_LINK=tcp, BLOOMERY_CARD_BUDGET=90G, BLOOMERY_HOST_LOCK=1); batch prefill, residency unset
unit: tok/s
predict: 25..40
condition: one binary (main after #1169), every arm's load its own process; the Spark's tier_server loop from the same
  tree, caches dropped before the sitting, free >= 100 GB; the plain arm's plan line names its nvtier bytes (the room
  is binding) or the arm is not the configuration; 4 rounds; rows marked [cold] stand with their mark
decide-in: the node arm inside 25..40 and the plain arm under 13: the headline "a 192 GB workstation + Spark" with both
  numbers and the ratio, conditions named; the prefill row (pp) beside it, read against the nodeprefill round
decide-below: the node arm under 25: the smaller host room moved something else (host_slots, residency) — read the
  lever records; the plain arm over 13: residency keeps more than 75 % hot, the model's hot share moves
decide-above: the node arm over 40: the host leg at plan a was larger than modelled; write it and re-read the plan (a)
  sitting's host wait
